// @title   TropeLang Concept Library — Existential
// @version 1.3
// @note    Fundamental conditions of conscious existence. These are the deep
//          questions all narrative traditions address regardless of culture.
//
// Import: import "trl/concepts/existential.trl"


// =============================================================================
// MORTALITY AND TIME
// =============================================================================

// The condition of being subject to death; all living things that can die are mortal.
// Mortality is not the same as being near death — it is the permanent structural possibility of death.
concept mortality      [%Timeless] [+Inevitable] [+Abstract]

// The condition of being not subject to death; exemption from natural ending.
// Immortality carries its own costs — it outlasts all bonds and contexts.
concept immortality    [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Paradoxical]

// The total span of a being's existence — the arc from beginning to ending.
// A life is both lived and narrated; the story of a life may differ from its experience.
concept life           [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The permanent cessation of a being's existence; the end of a life.
// Death is irreversible. Its meaning — punishment, release, tragedy, honor — varies by context.
concept death          [%Timeless] [+Inevitable] [+Irreversible] [+Abstract]

// The dimension in which events occur and change is possible; the medium of experience.
// Time is both measurable (duration) and subjective (how it feels).
concept time           [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Inevitable]

// The continued existence of effects, memories, or patterns beyond their origin.
// Continuity connects the past to the present to the future.
concept continuity     [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The condition of being temporary; all things that exist in time will end.
// Impermanence is not a flaw but a structural property of existence.
concept impermanence   [%Timeless] [+Inevitable] [+Abstract]


// =============================================================================
// IDENTITY AND SELF
// =============================================================================

// The continuous or coherent sense of being a particular, distinct entity.
// Identity may be given (birth, body, lineage) or constructed (choice, role, story).
concept identity       [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Fragile]

// The boundary between one entity and all others; the membrane of self.
// The self is experienced from within; it is constructed partly by others.
concept self           [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The entity or existence that is not-self; the world beyond one's own boundary.
// Other is necessary for self — without difference, identity dissolves.
concept other          [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The movement from one state of being to another at a fundamental level.
// Transformation is deeper than change — it alters the nature of the thing, not just its condition.
concept transformation [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Irreversible]

// The process of being shaped, formed, or developed — becoming what one will be.
// Becoming implies incompleteness; the subject is not yet what it is moving toward.
concept becoming       [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The question of whether one's actions are the product of one's own agency or prior causes.
// Free will is both a philosophical problem and a practical narrative necessity.
concept free_will      [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Paradoxical]


// =============================================================================
// SUFFERING AND TRANSCENDENCE
// =============================================================================

// The experience of pain, difficulty, or distress — physical, psychological, or existential.
// Suffering is not always visible from outside the sufferer.
concept suffering      [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [#Painful]

// The movement beyond ordinary limits — of self, pain, circumstance, or understanding.
// Transcendence may be spiritual, philosophical, artistic, or interpersonal.
concept transcendence  [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Aspirational]

// The state of coming to terms with what is — accepting conditions without denial.
// Acceptance is not the same as approval or resignation.
concept acceptance     [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The absence of apparent order, reason, or justice in events.
// Absurdity arises when expectations of meaning meet an indifferent reality.
concept absurdity      [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Paradoxical]

// The condition in which existence offers no apparent ground, center, or meaning.
// Void may be terrifying, liberating, or simply empty.
concept void           [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The question of why something exists rather than nothing; the ground of existence.
// Being underlies all other concepts; without it, nothing else applies.
concept being          [%Timeless] [+Primordial] [+Abstract]
